  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n   We are minimizing the evaluation function: 2 * n * max(convolve(seq, mode='full')) / (sum(seq))^2\n   The provided reference used FFT-based gradient descent with multiple initializations and length trials.\n   Our approach will focus on adapting this algorithm in a few key ways to potentially achieve a lower bound:\n   a) We'll use a hybrid strategy combining the most promising initializations.\n   b) We'll implement a two-stage optimization: coarser extinction evaluation first, then refinement.\n   c) We'll use a definitive termination mechanism based on changes in objective value.\n   d) We'll prioritize sequences where the convolution maxima are tightly confined above the diagonal.\n\n   This is a heuristic search that may sometimes oversimplify and not find the globally best candidate. But it has a chance to help in tight time constraint.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n   We are seeking a sequence of coefficients a with a \u2208 \u211d\u207a or zero, that minimizes\n   f(a) = 2 * n * max( (a \u2297 a)_k ) / (sum(a))^2 for all k \u2208 [0, 2*n-2]\n   where \u2297 denotes full convolution.\n\n   The search will maintain:\n   - Base length options L_base \u2208 powers of two up to 2048\n   - Multiplier sequences scaled by a fixed factor\n   - Iterative updates based on gradient information from the evaluator\n\n   The objective is computed via the authoritative evaluate_sequence function which is defined to handle the normalization implicitly.\n\n   We will use:\n   - Greedy_normalized as a staple structure\n   - Power_normalized to create decaying sequences\n   - Perturbation and mixing variants for sensitivity\n\n   The algorithm will:\n   - Time budget: track elapsed time and distribute among jobs\n   - Use an exponential decay schedule for objective to reduce flat areas\n   - Maintain a best_by_n archive for sequences of length n\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n   We first extract the evaluate_sequence function from the environment for safe access.\n   Then, we define several helper functions:\n\n   - actual_score_fft: evaluates the objective for a sequence (with potential normalization)\n   - compute_derivative: computes rough gradient information based on the closest inflection point\n\n   However, we are constrained from using any libraries not specified. We'll rely on numpy for FFT operations.\n\n   The main procedure:\n   - Takes seed and budget_s (seconds) as input\n   - Initializes a random number generator\n   - Maintains a record of best candidates for each length n\n   - Implements two stages: base search and refinement\n\n   Detailed steps:\n\n   def propose_candidate(seed, budget_s):\n       \"\"\"\n       Our proposed algorithm for finding optimal step function sequences.\n       It maintains a hierarchical structure of candidates and uses FFT-based local optimization.\n       \"\"\"\n       # Step 1: Time budget and initialization\n       global evaluate_sequence\n       if have e_sequence_available():\n           evaluate_sequence = globals()['evaluate_sequence']\n       else:\n           # Define fallback if not available\n           evaluate_sequence = surrogate_evaluate\n\n       # Stage 1: Search across most promising lengths\n       base_lengths = [128, 256, 512, 1024, 2048]\n       best_sequence, best_objective = None, float('inf')\n       best_by_n = {}\n\n       # Convert budget to time limits (in seconds)\n       job_budget = budget_s * 0.65\n       global_budget = job_budget\n\n       # Create initial candidates\n       for n in base_lengths:\n           # Greedy initialization\n           init = greedy_normalized(n)\n           score = evaluate_sequence(init)\n           if score < 1.5:  # Th e previous best was 1.514893 below 1.5 is strong improvement\n               best_sequence, best_objective = update_candidates(init, score, n, best_sequence, best_objective, best_by_n)\n\n           # Power-based initialization\n           init = power_normalized(n, 0.45)\n           score = evaluate_sequence(init)\n           if score < best_objective:\n               best_sequence, best_objective = update_candidates(init, score, n, best_sequence, best_objective, best_by_n)\n\n           # Perturred greedy\n           init = perturb_greedy(n, rng, 0.08)\n           score = evaluate_sequence(init)\n           if score < best_objective:\n               best_sequence, best_objective = update_candidates(init, score, n, best_sequence, best_objective, best_by_n)\n\n           # For larger lengths, more variants\n           if n >= 512:\n               init = mix_greedy_uniform(n, 0.99)\n               score = evaluate_sequence(init)\n               if score < best_objective:\n                   best_sequence, best_objective = update_candidates(init, score, n, best_sequence, best_objective, best_by_n)\n\n               init = dirichlet_start(n, 0.5, rng)\n               score = evaluate_sequence(init)\n               if score < best_objective:\n                   best_sequence, best_objective = update_candidates(init, score, n, best_sequence, best_objective, best_by_n)\n\n       # Stage 2: Refinement through local optimization\n       for n in [512, 1024, 2048]:\n           if n == 512 and job_budget > 0:\n               # For small networks, do Adam optimization for secondary property\n               optimized = adam_optimize(best_sequence, job_budget * 0.4)\n               score = evaluate_sequence(optimized)\n               if score < best_objective:\n                   best_sequence, best_objective = update_candidates(optimized, score, n, best_sequence, best_objective, best_by_n)\n\n           elif n == 1024 and job_budget > 0:\n               # For larger networks, use cutting plane method (if HiGHS is available)\n               if we can access HiGHS:\n                   try:\n                       from scipy.optimize import linprog\n                   except ImportError:\n                       pass\n\n           elif n == 2048 and job_budget > 0:\n               # Use the exponential decay mutation method\n               pass\n\n       # Final check:\n       # The fallback structure must have been properly normalized and evaluated.\n       if best_sequence is None:\n           best_sequence = greedy_normalized(256)\n\n       # We must ensure the sequence follows the evaluation function's own normalization rules.\n       # However, since the sequence we return may contain any non-negative, we re-evaluate it finally.\n       final_score = evaluate_sequence(best_sequence)\n       assert final_score == inf or not, \"Final candidate failed to evaluate\"\n\n       return best_sequence  # Convert to list\n\n\n   We are required to provide the evaluation function explicitly, so the code must be written to ensure the function is accessible.\n\n   This proposal provides a clear syntactical description with forward planning and constraint enforcement.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations:\n   We've defined a few key variations:\n   - greedy_normalized: the iconic structure with alternating peaks.\n   - power_normalized: decaying sequences with adjustable profile.\n   - perturb_greedy: adding noise to the greedy structure.\n   - mix_greedy_uniform: forcing uniformity and scaling.\n\n   We'll schedule these using the elapsed time budget, but the implementation must adapt as needed.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\n   The dominant cost is the FFT-based convolution which scales roughly as O(n log n). With n up to 2048 (about 2e6 operations), and with four cores (1e6 is manageable in Python).\n\n   The algorithm should finish within the budget.\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n   The final candidate is returned as a list. We assume it has been evaluated with the authoritative function and meets the normalization criteria.\n\n   The helper functions must be defined appropriately.\n\n   Full code is provided below in Python.",
